(; constants used by Mathlib.Data.Nat.Factorization.Induction, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: A commutative monoid is a monoid with commutative `(*)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommMonoid ;)
CommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (h : Proof (Eqt Set x x1)) -> (b : Elem x1) -> Elem x.

(; doc: `prod f g` is the product of `g a (f a)` over the support of `f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.prod ;)
Finsupp__prod : (f : Elem (Finsupp x M)) -> (g : (Elem x -> Elem M -> N)) -> Elem N.

(; doc: `m` and `n` are coprime, or relatively prime, if their `gcd` is 1. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.Coprime ;)
Nat__Coprime : (m : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Prop.

(; doc: `n.factorization` is the finitely supported function `ℕ →₀ ℕ` mapping each prime factor of `n` to its multiplicity in `n`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Nat.factorization ;)
Nat__factorization : (n : Elem Nat) -> Elem (Finsupp Nat Nat).

(; //LEAN:Nat.prime_two ;)
Nat__prime_two : Proof (prime 2).

(; //LEAN:Nat.recOnPosPrimePosCoprime._proof_1 ;)
Nat__recOnPosPrimePosCoprime___proof_1 : (motive : (Elem Nat -> Set)) -> (a : Elem Nat) -> (p : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (ha1 : Proof (Eq a 1)) -> Proof (Eqt Set (motive (times (pow p n) a)) (motive (times (pow p n) 1))).

(; //LEAN:Nat.recOnPosPrimePosCoprime._proof_2 ;)
Nat__recOnPosPrimePosCoprime___proof_2 : (motive : (Elem Nat -> Set)) -> (p : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> Proof (Eqt Set (motive (times (pow p n) 1)) (motive (pow p n))).

(; //LEAN:Nat.recOnPosPrimePosCoprime._proof_3 ;)
Nat__recOnPosPrimePosCoprime___proof_3 : (p : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (hp' : Proof (prime p)) -> (hn : Proof (Lt 0 n)) -> Proof (Lt 1 (pow p n)).

(; //LEAN:Nat.recOnPosPrimePosCoprime._proof_4 ;)
Nat__recOnPosPrimePosCoprime___proof_4 : (a : Elem Nat) -> (p : Elem Nat) -> (hpa : Proof (not (divide p a))) -> (ha1 : Proof (not (Eq a 1))) -> Proof (Lt 1 a).

(; //LEAN:Nat.recOnPosPrimePosCoprime._proof_5 ;)
Nat__recOnPosPrimePosCoprime___proof_5 : (a : Elem Nat) -> (p : Elem Nat) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (hp' : Proof (prime p)) -> (hpa : Proof (not (divide p a))) -> (hn : Proof (Lt 0 n)) -> Proof (Nat__Coprime (pow p n) a).

(; doc: "Dependent" if-then-else, normally written via the notation `if h : c then t(h) else e(h)`, is sugar for `dite c (fun h => t(h)) (fun h => e(h))`, and it is the same as `if c then t else e` except that `t` is allowed to depend on a proof `h : c`, and `e` can depend on `h : ¬c`. (Both branches use the same name for the hypothesis, even though it has different types in the two cases.)  We use this to be able to communicate the if-then-else condition to the branches. For example, `Array.get arr i h` expects a proof `h : i < arr.size` in order to avoid a bounds check, so you can write `if h : i < arr.size then arr.get i h else ...` to avoid the bounds check inside the if branch. (Of course in this case we have only lifted the check into an explicit `if`, but we could also use this proof multiple times or derive `i < arr.size` from some other proposition that we are checking in the `if`.)  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if h : c then t else e` in identifiers is `dite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:dite ;)
dite : (c : Prop) -> (t : (Proof c -> x)) -> (e : (Proof (not c) -> x)) -> Elem x.

